--
N5008 [expr.prim.req.compound]/1-2, [temp.constr.atomic]: `{ t.begin() }
-> pointer_like;' requires `t.begin()' to be a valid expression whose type
satisfies pointer_like; for T = int the substitution fails, so has_begin<int>
is false.  The front end treats it as satisfied (the static_assert fails):
the parser's fast path for a requires-expression whose body is a single
compound requirement with a type-constraint ("Try compound requirement: {
{ expr } -> concept<type>; }" in parse.cpp) builds a requirement without
its operand, which is taken as satisfied.  Removing the fast path is the
fix, but it currently breaks cpp20_ranges_basic: take_view / ref_view
begin() are resolved while their class is being defined and find the
customization-point object `begin' (see cpp20_ranges_upper_bound_array,
and the 152-line reduction in /tmp/r163/red/constraint_incomplete_class).
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept the program.  Pre-existing (fails on bin52).
Round 163m: passes.  The parser's compound-requirement fast path is removed
(a single `{ expr } -> concept;' requirement is parsed like any other
requirement body), member-qualified names are looked up in the object's
class only ([basic.lookup.qual.general]/2), and deduced-return members of
class template specializations are instantiated on use.  Fails on bin53.
